-- Generated by tools/mkinstaller.lua from installer/src; edit those files.
--
-- One file, two stages:
--
--   stage0  run as a program on an OS that gives programs component and
--           computer (OpenOS and others). It copies this file to the target
--           disk as /init.lua, keeps the old /init.lua, and restarts into
--           it. Nothing is erased yet.
--
--             nexus-install [<address|label>] [--from <dir|url>] [-y] ...
--
--   stage1  booted as /init.lua, without an OS. The installer itself: it
--           asks for the disk, hostname, user and parts to install, takes
--           the files from an internet card (the release named by
--           dist/channel.lua) or from the bundled copy in /.nexus-install,
--           unpacks the system onto the disk and finally replaces /init.lua
--           with the Nexus boot loader. Cancelling puts the old /init.lua
--           back. An install medium (`lua tools/nximg.lua media`) boots
--           straight into this stage.

